If the key doesn't exist in the map, this will create a new `std::vector<int>` under that key. The inserted integer is 10 but you can replace it with any integer according to your requirements.

In terms of performance and memory usage, inserting elements at the beginning of vector may have a performance impact as all elements need to be shifted one place to the right. However, `std::vector`'s move constructor and assignment operator are usually optimized for such operations. Moreover, if this is part of a high-performance server application where frequent updates and lookups are expected, it could be necessary to consider other data structures or strategies for optimizing memory usage.

