public class DataLoader<T> where T : class
{
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, Task<T>> cache = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, Task<T>>();

    public async Task<T> LoadAsync(string filePath)
    {
        try
        {
            if (!cache.TryGetValue(filePath, out var task))
            {
                task = CreateLoadingTask(filePath);
                cache[filePath] = task;
            }

            return await task;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is IOException || ex is UnauthorizedAccessException)
        {
            // Handle IO and security related exceptions here...
            throw;
        }
        catch (JsonReaderException jsonEx)
        {
            // Handle JSON parsing exceptions here...
            throw new ArgumentException("Invalid file format", nameof(filePath), jsonEx);
        }
        catch (InvalidCastException invalidEx)
        {
            // Handle XML deserialization exceptions here...
            throw new ArgumentException("Invalid data structure in the file", nameof(filePath), invalidEx);
        }
    }

    private async Task<T> CreateLoadingTask(string filePath)
    {
        var extension = Path.GetExtension(filePath).ToLowerInvariant();
        switch (extension)
        {
            case ".json": return await LoadJsonAsync(filePath);
            case ".xml": return await LoadXmlAsync(filePath);
            // Add cases for other formats here...
            default: throw new ArgumentException("Unsupported file format", nameof(filePath));
        }
    }

    private async Task<T> LoadJsonAsync(string filePath)
    {
        using (var streamReader = new StreamReader(filePath, Encoding.UTF8))
        {
            var jsonContent = await streamReader.ReadToEndAsync();
            return JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<T>(jsonContent);
        }
    }

    private async Task<T> LoadXmlAsync(string filePath)
    {
        using (var streamReader = new StreamReader(filePath, Encoding.UTF8))
        {
            var serializer = new XmlSerializer(typeof(T));
            return await Task.Run(() => (T)serializer.Deserialize(streamReader));
        }
    }
}
```
To extend this method for other file formats, you would add additional cases to the `CreateLoadingTask` method and provide corresponding async loading methods. Note that this is a basic example without implementing concurrent access or garbage collection control - those are more complex topics and should be handled by a larger application framework.
']

['```java
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.reflect.Constructor;
import org.apache.commons.logging.Log;
import org.apache.commons.logging.LogFactory;

public class Deserializer {
    private static final Log logger = LogFactory.getLog(Deserializer.class);
    private boolean strictVerificationEnabled = false; //Default value

    public void setStrictVerificationEnabled(boolean strictVerificationEnabled) {
        this.strictVerificationEnabled = strictVerificationEnabled;
    }

    public <T> T deserialize(byte[] data, Class<T> expectedClass) {
        try (ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
             ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(bais)) {
            // Check the serialized class matches the expected one. 
            if (!strictVerificationEnabled && !expectedClass.isInstance(in.readObject())) {
                logger.warn("The deserialized object does not match the expected class");
                return null;
            }
            // Strict verification is enabled, perform thorough checks to validate the class hierarchy 
            if (strictVerificationEnabled) {
                Object readObject = in.readObject();
                Class<?> readClass = readObject.getClass();

                boolean isValidReadClass = expectedClass.equals(readClass);

                // If not, the class does not match or it has unexpected fields/methods
                if (!isValidReadClass) {
                    logger.warn("The deserialized object does not match the expected class");
                    return null;
                }
            }

            Constructor<T> ctor = expectedClass.getDeclaredConstructor();
            ctor.setAccessible(true);
            T instance = ctor.newInstance(); 

            // Reconstruct fields and methods of the deserialized object, including private ones
            Field[] declaredFields = expectedClass.getDeclaredFields();
            for (Field field : declaredFields) {
                field.setAccessible(true);
                Object value = in.readObject(); 
                field.set(instance, value);
            }

            return instance;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.error("Error deserializing object", e);
            return null;
        } finally {
            if (strictVerificationEnabled && failedDeserializationAttempts > 0) {
                // Release resources or fallback to a default behavior 
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Please note: This code assumes that the "Strict Verification Enabled" configuration setting is set before calling `deserialize`. If strict verification fails, it logs a warning and returns null. The class checks are implemented in the same way as your description above. However, I did not have enough information on how to handle classes with unexpected fields/methods, so the code just compares the expectedClass with readObject's class. Also note that this implementation might throw exceptions when deserializing private fields and methods, which are made accessible using `setAccessible(true)` for simplicity. In a production environment, you would want to handle these cases more carefully, perhaps by throwing an exception or simply skipping them altogether.
']

