#include <mysql/mysql.h>
#include <pthread.h>

MYSQL *conn;
int error = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void logError(const char* message) {
    // Implement your own logging mechanism here. This is just a placeholder.
}

int initDBConnection(char *server, char *user, char *password, char *database) {
    conn = mysql_init(NULL);

    if (!mysql_real_connect(conn, server, user, password, database, 0, NULL, 0)) {
        logError(mysql_error(conn));
        error = 1;
    }
}

void runQuery(char *query) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (mysql_query(conn, query)) {
        logError(mysql_error(conn));
        error = 1;
    } else {
        MYSQL_RES *result = mysql_store_result(conn);

        int numRows = mysql_num_rows(result);
        // Process the result here...

        mysql_free_result(result);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code doesn't cover all of your requirements, but it shows how you could manage a database connection and run a query in a thread-safe way. 

To stream results, use `mysql_use_result` instead of `mysql_store_result` which fetches rows from the server as they come in rather than storing them all in memory. 

Memory management can be improved by using a connection pool to reuse connections and avoid unnecessary resource allocation/deallocation. Database driver choice (MySQL, PostgreSQL, etc) may also affect performance. The optimal database driver depends on specific requirements like support for different data types, transaction handling, etc.

For large datasets, consider implementing paging or batch processing to reduce memory usage. Use a suitable indexing strategy and tune your server's configuration to handle high loads. 

This implementation assumes that the existing logging framework has already been set up and can be used for logging errors using `logError` function.

Remember to always release resources after use to avoid leaks, so make sure you call `mysql_free_result(result);` after processing the result. Always check return values from API calls as they indicate success or failure, which should also be checked.
