['Below is an implementation of your task using Java's standard libraries, Apache Commons library for MD5 computation, Guava for caching and a pseudo-external service called 'ExternalHashingService'. Note that the actual implementation will depend on the specific external hashing service API you are using.

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheBuilder;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheLoader;
import com.google.common.cache.LoadingCache;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class HashProcessor {
    private final ExternalHashingService externalService = new ExternalHashingService();

    // Assuming the cache size is 10,000 and it can be adjusted based on your needs
    LoadingCache<String, String> cache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
      .maximumSize(10_000) 
      .build(new CacheLoader<String, String>(){
        public String load(String input) throws Exception {
          return computeHash(input);
       }
    });

    // Thread pool for managing external service requests
    ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10); 

    private final int MAX_SIZE = 2 * 1024; // 2KB

    public String processHashRequest(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) return "";

        try {
            return cache.get(input);
        } catch (ExecutionException e) {
            // Handle the exception here, for simplicity let's just print it out
            e.printStackTrace();
            return "";
        }
    }

    private String computeHash(String input) throws Exception {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) return "";

        // For small strings, use the standard library
        if (input.length() < MAX_SIZE) {
            return DigestUtils.md5Hex(input);
        } 
        // Otherwise offload to an external service
        else {
            Future<String> future = executor.submit(() -> externalService.computeHash(input));

            try {
                return future.get();
            } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
                // Handle exception gracefully here
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                throw new RuntimeException("Failed to compute hash", e);
            }
        }
    }
}
``` 
Please replace 'ExternalHashingService' and its method with actual external hashing service implementation. Also, in a real world scenario, you would have to add the proper error handling mechanisms for edge cases like service unavailability, timeouts etc., which are not covered here due to simplicity of this example. The cache is used to avoid computing hash for strings that were already computed before. This can save lots of CPU usage if the same string is processed multiple times.
