['Here is an example of how you might implement some parts of this service using Spring Boot:

```java
import org.apache.commons.codec.binary.Base64;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.util.DigestUtils;

import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

@Service
public class MD5HashingService {
    private final int maxAllowedLength = 1024 * 1024; // 1MB

    @Cacheable(value = "md5Hash", key = "#inputs")
    public String getMD5HashInFormat(String format, String... inputs) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        if (Arrays.stream(inputs).mapToInt(String::length).sum() > maxAllowedLength) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Inputs exceeded maximum allowed length");
        }

        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        for (String input : inputs) {
            md.update(input.getBytes());
        }

        byte[] digest = md.digest();

        switch (format) {
            case "hex":
                return DigestUtils.md5DigestAsHex(digest);
            case "base64":
                return Base64.encodeBase64String(digest);
            case "bytes":
                return Arrays.toString(digest);
            default:
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid format");
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we use Spring's cache abstraction to store the MD5 hashes of strings in memory. The `@Cacheable` annotation automatically checks if the result is already in the cache before computing it again. 

