#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <sys/time.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>

typedef struct {
    long offset;
    int key;
    atomic_double timestamp;
} Record;

// Define the global array and its size
Record** records = NULL;
size_t capacity = 0, size = 0;

// Define a mutex to ensure thread safety
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void insert(long offset, int key) {
    // Acquire the mutex before modifying data
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (size == capacity) {
        capacity = capacity ? capacity * 2 : 1;
        records = realloc(records, capacity * sizeof(Record*));
    }

    struct timeval tv;
    gettimeofday(&tv, NULL);

    Record* record = malloc(sizeof(Record));
    record->offset = offset;
    record->key = key;
    atomic_store(&record->timestamp, (double) tv.tv_sec + ((double)tv.tv_usec / 1000000));

    records[size++] = record;

    // Release the mutex after modifying data
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```

In this code:
- `insert` function is responsible for inserting a new record into the array. 
- It first acquires the lock before modifying the data, to prevent other threads from accessing it concurrently.
- If there's not enough space in the array, it doubles its capacity using `realloc`.
- It calculates the current time and inserts a new record into the array.
- Finally, it releases the lock after modifying data to allow other threads to access it. 

This is just an example and real implementation would need a lot more considerations (like error checking, efficient memory management, integration with external systems, etc.) as per your requirements. It might be beneficial to break down this problem into smaller tasks and tackle them one by one rather than trying to create a single comprehensive solution right off the bat.
