["Here's a skeleton implementation of what you asked for. Note that this is a complex task and does not cover all aspects requested here. It's also important to note that C does not have garbage collection or exceptions, so error handling must be done manually.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <assert.h>

typedef struct {
    long offset;
    int key;
    double timestamp;
} record;

static const size_t MAX_MEMORY = 100 * 1024 * 1024; // 100MB
static pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static size_t memory_usage = 0;

void* allocate(size_t n) {
    assert((n * sizeof(record)) <= MAX_MEMORY);

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    if (memory_usage + n > MAX_MEMORY) {
        // Adjust the number of records to fit within available memory.
        n = (MAX_MEMORY - memory_usage) / sizeof(record);
    }
    void* ptr = malloc(n * sizeof(record));
    if (!ptr) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        // Handle allocation failure.
        return NULL; 
    }
    memory_usage += n * sizeof(record);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        record* r = &((record*)ptr)[i];
        // Initialize offset and key as needed.
        r->timestamp = time(NULL); 
    }
    return ptr;
}

void deallocate(void* ptr, size_t n) {
    assert((n * sizeof(record)) <= MAX_MEMORY);

    free(ptr);

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    memory_usage -= n * sizeof(record); 
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}
```
In this code, `allocate` and `deallocate` are thread-safe functions that manage allocating and deallocating the records. If a record allocation fails due to memory constraints, it tries to adjust the number of records to fit within available memory. The usage statistics are kept in a shared variable (memory_usage) protected by a mutex.

This code does not implement a mechanism for queueing requests or handle shutdowns, and it does not optimize for speed/latency or cache performance - those would require a more detailed understanding of the expected workload and access patterns. It also doesn't include any enhancements for future requirements.
