["Here is a basic implementation of your problem using C11's atomic operations, pthread mutexes, and pthread condition variables for the concurrency control part. Note that in this scenario, we don't use any thread-safe linked list library like glib or even the Linux kernel's own list implementation due to their complexity and heavy overhead.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct DeviceStatus {
    int deviceId;
    atomic_int timestamp;
    atomic_int rssi;
} DeviceStatus;

typedef struct Node {
    struct Node *next;
    DeviceStatus *status;
} Node;

typedef struct LinkedList {
    Node *head;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
    pthread_cond_t cond;
} LinkedList;

LinkedList list = {.head = NULL, .lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER, .cond = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER};

void addDeviceStatus(int deviceId, int timestamp, int rssi) {
    Node *node = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    node->status = malloc(sizeof(DeviceStatus));
    node->status->deviceId = deviceId;
    atomic_store(&node->status->timestamp, timestamp);
    atomic_store(&node->status->rssi, rssi);

    pthread_mutex_lock(&list.lock);
    node->next = list.head;
    list.head = node;
    pthread_cond_signal(&list.cond); // Notify waiting threads that new data is available
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&list.lock);
}

void* pruneThread(void *arg) {
    int ageThreshold = *((int*) arg);

    while (1) {
        Node *prevNode = NULL;
        Node *node = list.head;

        pthread_mutex_lock(&list.lock);
        // Wait until new data is available or we timeout after the age threshold has passed
        struct timespec ts;
        clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &ts);
        ts.tv_sec += ageThreshold;

        while (node == list.head && pthread_cond_timedwait(&list.cond, &list.lock, &ts) == 0) {
            node = list.head;
        }

        // Prune old data
        int currentTime = time(NULL);
        while (node != NULL) {
            if (atomic_load(&node->status->timestamp) + ageThreshold < currentTime) {
                if (prevNode == NULL) {
                    list.head = node->next; // If we're at the head, move the head forward
                } else {
                    prevNode->next = node->next; // Otherwise skip over this node in our list
                }

                free(node->status);
                free(node);
            }

            if (prevNode == NULL) {
                node = list.head;
            } else {
                node = prevNode->next;
            }
        }

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&list.lock);
    }

    return NULL;
}
```
This is just a very basic and naive implementation. It can be optimized in various ways:

1. The code assumes that the timestamp value is only incrementing (not decrementing or resetting), which may not always be true for certain use cases. More complex logic could be implemented to handle those scenarios correctly.
2. This does not take into account system memory usage, so if you have a very high device count and/or large `DeviceStatus` structures, you might run out of memory. In real-world applications this would need to be handled more carefully.
3. The pruning mechanism is relatively simple: it only checks the oldest entry's timestamp against the current time. A more complex strategy could potentially reduce latency without having to scan through the entire list.
4. Error handling is minimal and may need to be expanded in a real-world application. 
5. This does not handle potential race conditions where multiple threads are attempting to add at the same time, or when there is only one thread but many `addDeviceStatus()` calls being made from different places within the codebase. It might be better if each part of your program could call a function with the needed parameters instead of directly calling this function itself.

Also note that these are just hints on how to handle such scenarios and don't represent a fully functional or optimized solution, they need further adjustments based on specific use case details.
