['Here's a simplified version of what could be one possible solution in C programming language. Note that this does not include all the aspects like memory pool, atomic operations, integration with other parts of the system or detailed error handling and logging mechanisms, which would greatly complicate the code base. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    double value;
    char *buffer;
} Entry;

Entry *entries = NULL;
size_t entriesSize = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

char* generateRandomString(int length) {
    static char charset[] = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789";
    char *randomString;

    randomString = malloc(length + 1);

    if (randomString != NULL) {
        for (int n = 0;n < length; ++n) {
            int key = rand() % (int)(sizeof charset - 1);
            randomString[n] = charset[key];
        }

        randomString[length] = '\0';
    }

    return randomString;
}

void initEntry(double min, double max) {
    srand((unsigned int)time(NULL));

    Entry *newEntries = realloc(entries, (entriesSize + 1) * sizeof(Entry));

    if(newEntries != NULL){
        entries = newEntries;
        ++entriesSize;
    }

    entries[entriesSize - 1].value = min + ((double)rand() / RAND_MAX) * (max - min);
    int bufferLength = 1 + rand() % 20;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    entries[entriesSize - 1].buffer = generateRandomString(bufferLength);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void deleteEntry(size_t index) {
    if (index >= entriesSize) return;

    free(entries[index].buffer);

    for (size_t i = index; i < entriesSize - 1; ++i) {
        entries[i] = entries[i + 1];
    }

    --entriesSize;
}

void updateEntry(size_t index, double value, const char *buffer){
    if (index >= entriesSize) return;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    entries[index].value = value;
    free(entries[index].buffer);
    size_t bufferLength = strlen(buffer) + 1;
    entries[index].buffer = malloc(bufferLength);

    if (entries[index].buffer != NULL){
        strncpy(entries[index].buffer, buffer, bufferLength);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
In this code:
- `Entry` is a struct to store the double value and char pointer.
- The `initEntry()` function creates a new entry with random values within the given range, allocates memory for the string buffer and sets it using a helper function `generateRandomString()`.
- The `deleteEntry()` function frees up the memory used by a particular entry and removes it from the array.
- The `updateEntry()` function updates an existing entry with new values. Note that locking is necessary to ensure thread safety when updating entries as shared resources (the array of Entry) can be accessed concurrently.
- `pthread_mutex_t` is used for mutual exclusion, which means only one thread can access the critical section at a time. This prevents data corruption and ensures that changes are accurately reflected in the array.

This code doesn't include all the requested aspects mentioned above, but it provides a good starting point for building more complex systems. You should also consider error checking when dealing with dynamic memory allocation, especially when using `realloc()` or `malloc()`. 

Remember to call `pthread_mutex_init(&lock, NULL);` before you start any other operation that uses the lock. And finally make sure to destroy the mutex after use by calling `pthread_mutex_destroy(&lock)`.
