['Here's an example C# class `DynamicValueGenerator` that follows most of your requirements but it's worth noting that some parts are omitted due to complexity for brevity. This includes the dynamic update mechanism from external configuration service, network interruptions handling and real-time updates, high load conditions management, etc. Also, this example doesn't include parsing config file or logging mechanism, which should be added based on specific project requirements.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading;

public class DynamicValueGenerator<T> where T : IComparable<T> {
    private readonly ReaderWriterLockSlim _lock = new();
    private List<(T Lower, T Upper)> _ranges = new();

    public void AddRange(T lower, T upper) {
        _lock.EnterWriteLock();
        try {
            if (_ranges.Any(range => range.Lower.CompareTo(upper) <= 0 && range.Upper.CompareTo(lower) >= 0)) {
                throw new ArgumentException("New range overlaps with existing ranges");
            }
            _ranges.Add((lower, upper));
        } finally {
            _lock.ExitWriteLock();
        }
    }

    public void RemoveRange(T lower, T upper) {
        _lock.EnterWriteLock();
        try {
            _ranges.RemoveAll(range => range.Lower.CompareTo(lower) == 0 && range.Upper.CompareTo(upper) == 0);
        } finally {
            _lock.ExitWriteLock();
        }
    }

    public T GenerateValue() {
        var rnd = new Random();

        _lock.EnterReadLock();
        try {
            if (_ranges.Count == 0) throw new InvalidOperationException("No ranges defined");

            var rangeIndex = rnd.Next(_ranges.Count);
            dynamic lowerBound = _ranges[rangeIndex].Lower;
            dynamic upperBound = _ranges[rangeIndex].Upper;

            if (typeof(T) == typeof(int)) {
                return (T) Convert.ChangeType(rnd.Next((int)lowerBound, (int)upperBound + 1), typeof(T));
            } else if (typeof(T) == typeof(float)) {
                return (T) Convert.ChangeType((float) rnd.NextDouble() * ((float) upperBound - (float) lowerBound) + (float) lowerBound, typeof(T));
            }

            throw new NotSupportedException("Unsupported type");
        } finally {
            _lock.ExitReadLock();
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, `AddRange` method adds a range and throws an exception if the range overlaps with any existing ranges. `RemoveRange` removes all ranges that match the specified lower and upper bounds. `GenerateValue` generates a random value within one of the defined ranges. The class uses `ReaderWriterLockSlim` for thread safety, it is a useful construct in scenarios where shared resources are read more often than written to, ensuring that multiple threads can read from these resources simultaneously without causing locks or deadlocks.
']

['This is a complex task that cannot be implemented in a single code block due to its numerous functionalities and requirements. Here's an example of how you might begin implementing such a system using C. This code includes struct definitions for 'event', memory management functions with error handling, dynamic resizing strategy and reading integers from standard input. However, it does not include all the features requested in your task:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#define INIT_SIZE 10
#define RESIZE_FACTOR 2

typedef struct {
    long offset;
    char* message;
    int eventType;
    double timestamp;
} Event;

int totalEvents = 0;
Event* events = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void resize() {
    if (events == NULL) {
        events = malloc(sizeof(Event) * INIT_SIZE);
        if (events == NULL) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Allocation failed.\n");
            exit(-1);
        }
        return;
    }
    int newSize = totalEvents * RESIZE_FACTOR;
    Event* temp = realloc(events, sizeof(Event) * newSize);
    if (temp == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Reallocation failed.\n");
        free(events);
        exit(-1);
    }
    events = temp;
}

void addEvent(long offset, char* message, int eventType) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (totalEvents % INIT_SIZE == 0) {
        resize();
    }
    Event newEvent;
    newEvent.offset = offset;
    newEvent.message = strdup(message);  // duplicates message to avoid sharing memory
    if (newEvent.message == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Allocation for message failed.\n");
        exit(-1);
    }
    newEvent.eventType = eventType;
    newEvent.timestamp = time(NULL);  // set timestamp to current time
    events[totalEvents++] = newEvent;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void cleanup() {
    for (int i = 0; i < totalEvents; ++i) {
        free(events[i].message);  // frees message duplicated with strdup
    }
    free(events);
    events = NULL;
    totalEvents = 0;
}

void readAndAdd() {
    int n;
    while (scanf("%d", &n) == 1) {
        addEvent(n, "Message", 0);
    }
}
```

In this example, `addEvent` is thread-safe as it uses a mutex to lock and unlock the shared data structure. The memory management functions handle potential allocation failures by checking if `malloc` or `realloc` return NULL and exiting if they do. The memory cleanup function `cleanup` frees all allocated memory.

Please note that this is a simplified example, implementing all requested features would require a more comprehensive solution and likely involving additional libraries such as pthreads for thread-safe operations, glib for dynamic resizing strategy, or a configuration file parser library. It might also involve integration with an external logging service, which you'd have to implement separately.
