['Here's an example of how you might approach this problem in C. This implementation uses a doubly linked list to manage the nodes and a hash table to map strings to their respective nodes. Eviction policy is LRU (Least Recently Used) and implemented using a doubly linked list where the least recently accessed node will be at the tail of the list. The eviction process involves traversing from tail until enough space has been freed up or all nodes have been considered for deletion.

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not take into account every edge case, nor does it handle errors gracefully. It's also important to consider the implications of using a custom allocator on an existing application framework. Depending on how your existing codebase is structured and what services you have available from your frameworks, you might need to adapt this approach to work within your constraints.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct Node {
    char* string;
    int priority;
    struct Node *next, *prev;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
Node* tail = NULL;

// A simple hash table to map strings to nodes
#define TABLE_SIZE 10000
Node* hashTable[TABLE_SIZE];

void addNode(char* str, int priority) {
    Node *node = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    node->string = strdup(str);
    node->priority = priority;

    // Add the new node to the tail of the list
    if (tail != NULL) {
        tail->next = node;
        node->prev = tail;
    } else {
        head = node;
    }
    tail = node;

    // Hash the string and add the node to the hash table
    size_t h = hash(str) % TABLE_SIZE;
    if (hashTable[h] != NULL) {
        printf("Collision!\n");
    }
    hashTable[h] = node;
}

void applyTransform(Node* node, void (*transform)(char*)) {
    transform(node->string);
}

void lruEviction() {
    Node *node = tail;

    // Traverse the list from tail to head and evict nodes until enough space has been freed up or all nodes have been considered for deletion.
    while (node != NULL) {
        free(node->string);

        size_t h = hash(node->string) % TABLE_SIZE;
        if (hashTable[h] == node) {
            hashTable[h] = NULL; // Remove the evicted node from the hash table.
        } else {
            printf("Collision when evicting!\n");
        }

        Node *prev = node->prev;
        free(node);
        if (prev != NULL) prev->next = NULL; // Update the tail of the list.

        node = prev;
    }
}

void transformStringToUppercase(char* str) {
    for (int i = 0; str[i]; i++)
        str[i] = toupper((unsigned char) str[i]);
}
```
In this code, `addNode` function is used to create a new node and add it to the list. `applyTransform` function is used to apply transformation on each node's string. `lruEviction` function is used to perform LRU eviction when memory limit is reached. A simple hash function (hash) for hashing strings is not shown, please implement as per your requirement. The code also doesn't include any error checking or handling mechanisms such as out of memory errors and the like. These would need to be added according to specific requirements of your use case.
']

