['Here is a simple implementation based on the requirements you provided. This code doesn't handle all edge cases that could occur in real life, but it will provide a good starting point for handling memory allocation and error handling in your program. It uses dynamic allocation to manage memory based on the number of incoming events and ensures that total memory usage doesn't exceed predefined limits.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <stdio.h>

// Definition of event structure
typedef struct {
    char *event_identifier;
    int event_type;
    time_t timestamp;
    float confidence_score;
} event;

// Function to handle memory allocation for an array of events
int allocate_events(size_t number_of_events, size_t max_memory, event **event_buffer) {

    // Validate input parameters
    if (number_of_events <= 0 || event_buffer == NULL) {
        printf("Invalid input parameter to allocate_events function\n");
        return -1;  // Return failure code
    }

    size_t total_size = number_of_events * sizeof(event);

    // Check if memory limit is exceeded
    if (total_size > max_memory) {
        printf("Memory limit exceeded. Required: %zu, Available: %zu\n", total_size, max_memory);
        return -1;  // Return failure code
    }

    // Dynamic allocation of memory for event buffer
    *event_buffer = malloc(total_size);
    if (*event_buffer == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        return -1;  // Return failure code
    }

    memset(*event_buffer, 0, total_size);  // Initialize memory to zero for safety

    return 0;  // Return success code
}
```
This function `allocate_events` takes the number of events and a pointer to an event buffer as input. It calculates the required size in bytes based on the number of events, checks if this exceeds the maximum memory limit and allocates memory accordingly. If allocation fails or the inputs are invalid, it prints an error message and returns -1 (a common return value for failure). Otherwise, it initializes the allocated memory to zero using `memset` and returns 0 (a common return value for success).

The caller of this function is responsible for freeing the allocated memory after use by calling `free(event_buffer)`. This ensures proper management of dynamic memory and minimizes potential memory leaks.
']

