For managing memory dynamically for user sessions, we would typically use dynamic memory allocation (malloc, calloc) or realloc. For efficient lookups and insertions/deletions in hash table data structure, libraries like glib provide thread-safe hash tables. 

Here is a simplified version of the code:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <glib.h>

typedef struct {
    char* username;
    time_t loginTimestamp;
    uint32_t sessionID;
    bool activeFlag;
    size_t lastActivityTimestamp;
} Session;

// Lock for thread safety
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

GHashTable* sessions;
size_t maxSessions = 100;  // default maximum number of sessions
char* configFilePath = "/path/to/config.txt";  // path to the configuration file

void loadConfig(const char *fileName) {
    FILE *file = fopen(fileName, "r");

    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Could not open config file: %s\n", fileName);
        exit(1);
    }

    // Read configurations from the file and set them
    char buffer[256];
    while (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), file)) {
        // Extract key-value pairs from each line in the config file
        char *key = strtok(buffer, "=");
        char *value = strtok(NULL, "=");

        if (strcmp(key, "maxSessions") == 0) {
            maxSessions = atoi(value);
        } else {
            printf("Invalid config option: %s\n", key);
            exit(1);
        }
    }

    fclose(file);
}

void* addSession(const char *username, uint32_t sessionID) {
    Session* session = malloc(sizeof(Session));
    if (session == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory for new session.\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    // Initialize the session data
    session->username = strdup(username);
    time(&session->loginTimestamp);  // set login timestamp from an external source
    session->sessionID = sessionID;
    session->activeFlag = true;
    session->lastActivityTimestamp = 0;  // default value or derived from session ID

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (g_hash_table_size(sessions) >= maxSessions) {
        printf("Maximum number of sessions reached.\n");
        free(session->username);
        free(session);
        return NULL;
    } else {
        g_hash_table_insert(sessions, GINT_TO_POINTER(sessionID), session);
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    printf("Session %d added for user %s.\n", sessionID, username);
    return session;
}
```
In this example, a Session struct is defined with the required fields. A hash table `sessions` stores pointers to these sessions, indexed by their session IDs (converted to void*). 

The function `loadConfig` reads configurations from a file and updates variables based on them. For simplicity, only the maximum number of sessions is configurable in this example. 

The function `addSession` creates a new Session for a given username and session ID, sets its login timestamp (from an external source) and other fields to their default or derived values, adds it to the hash table if there's space, and returns a pointer to the newly created session.

This is just a starting point. It does not include all the functionalities requested like efficient memory management, dynamic resizing of the hash table, handling external data source failures, logging, command-line interface, serialization/deserialization to persistent storage, or error handling for configuration file parsing and invalid configurations. 

