Log *head = NULL; // Head of the linked list (log entries)
pthread_mutex_t lock;
int maxEntries, currentEntries = 0;

void* addToList(char* message, time_t timestamp, Severity severity){
    Log* newLog = malloc(sizeof(Log)); // Allocate memory for the log entry

    if (newLog == NULL) {
        printf("Error allocating memory\n");
        return NULL; // Handle error: return or throw exception based on your needs
    }

    newLog->message = message;
    newLog->timestamp = timestamp;
    newLog->severity = severity;
    newLog->next = NULL;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Lock to ensure thread safety while modifying the linked list
    if (currentEntries >= maxEntries) {
        printf("Max entries reached. Resizing...\n"); 
        maxEntries *= 2; // Dynamically resize the log entries
        Log* newArray = realloc(head, maxEntries * sizeof(Log)); // Allocate more memory for the linked list
        if (newArray == NULL) {
            printf("Error resizing array\n");
            return NULL; // Handle error: return or throw exception based on your needs
        } else {
            head = newArray;
        }
    }

    Log* lastLog = head; 
    while(lastLog->next != NULL) // Traverse to the end of linked list
       lastLog = lastLog->next;
    lastLog->next = newLog; // Add log entry at the end of the linked list
    ++currentEntries;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock after modifying the linked list

    return NULL; // The function returns nothing as per your needs. This is just an example
}
```
This code creates a log entry with a message, timestamp and severity level which are then added to a linked list (head). It uses a lock so that threads don't interfere while modifying the linked list, and if it runs out of space, it doubles the size of memory allocated for it. 
Please note: This is just an example and the actual implementation will depend on many factors such as your specific requirements and constraints. You would also need to parse configuration files, handle disk space monitoring, timed logging operations, etc. that are not covered here. Also, C does not have built-in thread safety for dynamic memory allocation (like in languages like Java or Python) so you will need to implement these features manually.
