['This task requires multiple steps including opening a file, reading its contents line by line, parsing those lines for data and storing it in appropriate structures. Furthermore, we have to allocate memory dynamically to accommodate large number of elements (structures) in arrays while ensuring each element is unique and error handling is present.

Below is the C function that satisfies all these requirements:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#define MAX_ELEMS 1000
#define MAX_CHARS 100

typedef struct element {
    double value;
    char str[MAX_CHARS];
} element;

typedef struct summary {
    element *ptr;
} summary;

int readFile(char* filename, element arr[]) {
    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL){
        printf("Could not open the file!\n");
        return -1;
    }

    char line[MAX_CHARS];
    int count = 0;
    while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), file)) {
        element e;
        if (sscanf(line, "%lf,%99s", &e.value, e.str) == 2){
            arr[count] = e;
            count++;
            if(count >= MAX_ELEMS){
                break;
            }
        } else {
            printf("Line %d is malformed and is ignored\n", count + 1);
        }
    }

    fclose(file);
    return count;
}

int uniqueRandom(int lower, int upper) { 
    static int* used = NULL;
    static int max = 0;
    if (max < upper){
        free(used);
        max = upper;
        used = calloc(max, sizeof(int));
    }

    while (1){
        int r = lower + rand() % (upper - lower + 1); 
        if (!used[r]){ 
            used[r] = 1; 
            return r; 
        }
    } 
}

void generateSummary(element arr[], summary summ[], int numElements) {
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_ELEMS; ++i){
        summ[i].ptr = &arr[uniqueRandom(0,numElements-1)];
    }
}

void processFile(char* filename) {
    element *elements = malloc(MAX_ELEMS * sizeof(element));
    if (elements == NULL){
        printf("Could not allocate memory for elements!\n");
        return;
    }

    int numElements = readFile(filename, elements);
    if (numElements < 0) {
        free(elements);
        return;
    }

    summary *summaries = malloc(MAX_ELEMS * sizeof(summary));
    if (summaries == NULL){
        printf("Could not allocate memory for summaries!\n");
        free(elements);
        return;
    }

    generateSummary(elements, summaries, numElements);

    // Use the summaries array here... 

    free(summaries);
    free(elements);
}
```
The function `processFile` is used to read from a CSV file and process it. The function `readFile` reads lines from the file, parses them into data structures, and stores the results in an array of elements. If memory allocation fails or if the file cannot be opened, error messages are printed and -1 is returned.

The `generateSummary` function generates a summary for each element by assigning it a pointer to a randomly selected struct from the populated `element` array. The `uniqueRandom` function ensures that no duplicate `summary` pointers point to the same `struct element`. 

Remember that you need to free allocated memory after usage to prevent memory leakage and call `processFile("yourfile.csv")` with the name of your CSV file as argument. This solution is optimized for systems with limited resources, handles potential memory allocation failures, supports external data sources and can be easily extended in the future by adding more functions or altering existing ones to handle different types of files.
']

