['The problem you're asking is quite complex and may need a lot of development time, but here's a simplified C implementation of a function that processes files in a directory, checking for their existence, and appending a tag to their names if they don't exist. 

Please note this does not include all of the considerations you asked for, but it can be used as a starting point. Also, note that error handling is minimal here - in a real-world scenario, you should definitely add more checks and thorough logging.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dirent.h>
#include <string.h>

void process_files(const char* dirpath, const char* tag) {
    DIR *dir = opendir(dirpath);

    if (dir == NULL) {
        printf("Could not open directory: %s\n", dirpath);
        return;
    }

    struct dirent *entry;

    while ((entry = readdir(dir)) != NULL) {
        if (strcmp(entry->d_name, ".") == 0 || strcmp(entry->d_name, "..") == 0) 
            continue; // Skip current and parent directory entries.

        char filepath[1024]; // Assuming the path won't exceed this length.
        snprintf(filepath, sizeof(filepath), "%s/%s", dirpath, entry->d_name);

        FILE *f = fopen(filepath, "r"); 

        if (f == NULL) { // File does not exist - append the tag.
            char new_filename[1024];
            snprintf(new_filename, sizeof(new_filename), "%s/%s_%s", dirpath, entry->d_name, tag);

            if (rename(filepath, new_filename) != 0) {
                printf("Could not rename file: %s\n", filepath);
                continue; 
            }
        } else {
            fclose(f); // File exists - no need to append tag.
        }
    }

    closedir(dir);
}
```
This is a very simplified example and does not take into account all the considerations you asked for, including external data sources, system dependencies, and concurrency control. Implementing these features would require significant additional work and consideration of thread safety, atomicity, caching mechanisms, error handling and logging etc. 

